MEASUREMENT EVIDENCE for br-r37-c1-genadjdi (this commit landed without any).

DiGraph/generate_adjlist crosses parity: 0.8593x/0.8560x -> 1.1538x/1.1732x.

WHY IT LOST. generate_adjlist routed Graph to a native Rust line builder and
left DiGraph on a Python loop over _native_adjacency_keys(). Decomposed at
V=300/E=1200 (probe_genadj.py, same invocation):

    160.85us  nx    WHOLE list(generate_adjlist)
    189.00us  fnx   WHOLE list(generate_adjlist)
     67.98us    fnx stage1: _native_adjacency_keys() alone  = 42.3% of nx's
                             ENTIRE call
      8.53us    nx  stage1: G.adjacency() drained
    115.14us    fnx stage2: the Python format loop

fnx paid 8x networkx's whole-adjacency cost just to box
Vec<(PyObject, Vec<PyObject>)> before formatting one character. It WINS the
line-building half and lost the row purely on that snapshot, which is not
reachable from Python. The fix is the sibling's fix: give DiGraph the same
native line builder, gated on _native_has_succ_py_keys.

TWO HYPOTHESES MEASURED AND REFUTED, not assumed:
  - map(str, (node, *nbrs)) instead of two lists: SLOWER, 130.71us vs 108.74us.
  - the dead seen.add: networkx guards it with "if not directed", fnx's bulk
    path did not. Real but only 6.40us (3.4%) - cannot flip the row. Left in
    place; after this change DiGraph no longer reaches that path.

SUBSTRATE. Both arms built by me from the same clean Rust tree, differing only
by this hunk. The in-tree .so at the time was a peer's build with a DIFFERENT
sha, so it was not a valid before-arm. Arms are frozen .so copies, so later peer
churn cannot reach them.

