Headline row, V=300/E=1200: fnx 193.80/195.42us -> 140.92/139.41us against a
live networkx arm that stayed at 166.41/166.58 -> 162.06/163.55us. The nx arm
not moving is what makes this a win rather than a self-speedup.

CONTROLS ARE THE PROOF THE ARMS DIFFER ONLY BY THIS HUNK. MultiDiGraph is flat
to 0.0011 across all four passes. The Graph control's two BEFORE values (1.1371,
1.1887) BRACKET its two AFTER values (1.1832, 1.1556), so its spread is drift,
not arm skew. A one-sided move on the touched arm would have meant a real
effect; a two-sided non-move means artifact-free.

The V=2000 row is directionally right but noisy on the NX side (nx 1779us vs
2139us between passes) while fnx held 1189/1206us. No precise ratio is claimed
there.

CORRECTNESS.
  - Reachability: the native builder is DISPATCHED, not merely present -
    present=False on before, dispatched=True on after. A native method no
    caller reaches is invisible to timing.
  - Parity: 453 generate_adjlist shapes vs live networkx across 4 classes and 6
    delimiters (canonical-string collisions 1/1.0/True/"1", tuple and bool
    keys, self-loops, isolates, empty graphs, delimiter-in-node-name, empty
    string nodes, removal reordering): 0 divergences on the after arm.
  - 42/42 derived-container checks (subgraph, edge_subgraph, reverse view,
    reverse copy, nested subgraph) - these matter because rv.subgraph() returns
    an EXACT DiGraph, so the new gate routes it to the native builder.
  - New regression test FAILS on the unfixed arm and passes on the fixed one.

SIDE FINDINGS.
  - br-r37-c1-rt5q3: DiGraph.reverse(copy=False).subgraph() raises TypeError
    where networkx returns a view. Pre-existing, reproduced on an unmodified
    build; the native path incidentally routes around it.
  - MultiDiGraph/generate_adjlist measures 0.7995x and is now the worst loss on
    this surface - found by carrying it as a control.
